Rules, names, comments, tests, and idiom are checked elsewhere. Never report them.

Walk these hunts over every hunk in scope:
| Hunt | Look for |
|---|---|
| Boundaries | off-by-one, inclusive vs exclusive end, empty collection, first and last item |
| Absent values | nil, empty string, zero, missing map key, an optional not set |
| Error paths | an error dropped, logged and ignored, or turned into a success value |
| Concurrency | a new shared value read or written from two goroutines with no guard |
| Callers | a changed signature, return value, or meaning whose callers were not updated |
| Pairs | an open, register, lock, or mark whose close runs in a hook or callback of another layer; an exit of a caller up the stack that skips it |
| Ownership | a token, ID, or cursor that one owner (queue, subscription, process, user) issues and that another owner can pass back |
| Misuse | the likely caller mistake with a new value (`>` for `>=`, a stale value, a value of another owner), and whether it fails as a skip or as a duplicate |

Walk every hunt before you answer. Look for an input that makes the changed code give a wrong result.
Identify the affected component, its callers, and the outcome they require. Use the diff and repository evidence.
For a service, consider availability and persistent state across requests and restarts.
For CI, consider false passes, incorrect artifacts, exposure of credentials, and failures that block valid builds or releases.
Skip a temporary CI failure when the job reports it and one cheap, safe retry restores the intended outcome.
Check the input source, repeated work, and persistent effects before treating a retry as cheap.
Apply service expectations to a CI change when its effects reach a deployed service or a persistent runner.
Write a yes/no question only when you can name a plausible input and a material consequence for that component or its callers.
A YES answer must establish the defect.
Return an empty list if no question meets these conditions.
At most {{.MaxHypotheses}}. Prefer a silent wrong result over a loud failure. Merge two questions about one code path.
